WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
- Our next guests have been,

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.540
you've been with the
"Hunger Games" universe

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.560
for more than a decade,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.390
she's produced every single film,

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:08.580
he's directed all except
for the very first one.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.653
Let's welcome Nina Jacobson and
Francis Lawrence, everybody.

00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:14.653
(audience cheering)

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.020
I mean, when you said kind of
bye at the end of the last,

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.640
did you think you'd be coming back?

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.820
- No, I mean, when we made,
you know, these movies,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.050
it was a joy, honestly.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.910
It was an incredible experience,

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.580
because we loved the books
so much and we got so close.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.400
And so we really felt like it was over.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.560
You know, it was bittersweet.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.500
We felt proud and we felt we were done.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.020
- And also, I would say,
Suzanne said she was done.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.430
- Yeah.
- She'd been working

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.900
on the books, the movies, for 10 years,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.970
she wanted to go off, do other things.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:46.260
And four years later,

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.330
she called us up out of the
blue and said, "Guess what?

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.250
I'm almost done with a new book."

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.970
We're like, "What?"
- Yeah.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.210
- Yeah, and she wouldn't
tell us what it was about.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.180
She just said, "It's 64 years
before the first stories.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.580
There's one major crossover character,"

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.287
who we could start to piece
together who that was gonna be,

00:01:02.287 --> 00:01:04.830
"and that there's a huge
music element to it."

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:05.993
- [Kelly] Which I love.
- Yes.

00:01:05.993 --> 00:01:07.830
- I mean, I love the original ones,

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:08.663
but I'm like way more,

00:01:08.663 --> 00:01:09.496
and you've got music video

00:01:09.496 --> 00:01:10.329
background as well.
- Yeah.

00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:11.700
- I like we're both music world as well.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.430
So there's a lot of
like, the style in here,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.450
the way you were singing, Rachel,

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.566
first of all, is so beautiful.

00:01:16.566 --> 00:01:17.821
- Thank you.
- Your vibrato is woo.

00:01:17.821 --> 00:01:18.885
(audience cheering)

00:01:18.885 --> 00:01:20.718
But I love how you go.

00:01:21.801 --> 00:01:24.420
It's almost like singer-songwriter,

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.220
little country-folk in there.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.360
Like, was this all planned?

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.770
That vibe, that sound?

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.510
- Yeah, I mean, you could probably attest

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.150
to this better than I can,
but Suzanne used to be

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.400
a country music DJ.
- [Francis] Yes.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.687
- And so it was one of those things

00:01:37.687 --> 00:01:40.230
that she's been so immersed in that world.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:45.230
And, you know, District 12
geographically is North Carolina,

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.910
Appalachian, very southern.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:52.170
So that music was very like
jug band, folksy country music,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.750
and she had a clear
idea of what she wanted.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.970
All of the songs, she had
actually written out the lyrics

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.235
in the book what she
wanted them to sound like.

00:02:00.235 --> 00:02:01.410
- She told me I should

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.042
look at the Ken Burns country documentary.

00:02:03.042 --> 00:02:05.220
- Oh God, it's so good!
- And it's amazing.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.450
- Yeah.
- And it was in the pandemic,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.950
so I had a lot of time on my hands.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.410
So I watched all, I think, 16 hours.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.690
But what I fell in love with was that era

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.970
from the '20s and the '30s
with the Carter Family,

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.590
and with Maybelle Carter, and all of that,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.120
and "Wildwood Flower," and those songs.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.470
And I think that's kind of the era

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.150
that she was really
pulling from when thinking

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:22.470
of these ballads and these songs

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.871
that were passed down through generations.

00:02:24.871 --> 00:02:28.950
And one of the reasons Rachel
plays the big guitar too,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.000
the big Gibson,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.580
is we were basing that off
of Maybelle Carter's guitar

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:33.780
that she played.
- Oh, I love that

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.070
everything had purpose.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.060
Oh, that's beautiful.
- Exactly.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.580
Yeah, and then she just fit right in

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.490
and like got the tone
of it all so perfectly.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.890
And then, what I will say that was amazing

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.783
is that she sang live every day.

00:02:46.864 --> 00:02:48.083
(audience cheering)
- That's my next thing.

00:02:49.213 --> 00:02:51.030
That is my next thing.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.160
I heard that you did sing live,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.170
and you're a singer, but I just...

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.820
People don't get that you
have to do those takes over,

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:57.653
and over, and over again.

00:02:57.653 --> 00:02:59.447
Like, it's a lot to do that live.

00:02:59.447 --> 00:03:01.770
And so that's incredible, not everybody...

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.510
I don't know many people that'll do that.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.021
- I've never ever seen somebody do it.

00:03:06.021 --> 00:03:08.970
Every take live.
- Yeah, that's incredible.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.940
We have lots of "Hunger Games"
super fans in the audience,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.380
and there's one I wanna point out,

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.900
our assistant associate producer, Olivia.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.500
This is real, she actually
wrote an 8th grade paper

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.850
on how the "Hunger Games"

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.430
was adapted from the page to the screen.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.250
She even interviewed Nina for it.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.870
I mean, it's crazy.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.460
- It moves me, honestly.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.380
And to see somebody like Olivia

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.120
who was so inspired in 8th grade,

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.090
and then has held onto that
and brought it with her,

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:36.960
it really moves me.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.670
It means a lot to me.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.290
You know, we come to it as fans.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.380
But I have a very special
place in my heart for the fans,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.540
and to see people who grew up with it

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.270
and then turn out like
this is pretty exciting.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.490
- I gotta ask you, how
did you do on your report?

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.290
- I promise, I was an A plus student.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.700
I don't know what happened.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.560
I think my teacher took off
a few points and I ended up

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.260
getting an A minus on it.
- What?

00:03:58.447 --> 00:03:59.948
Let me see that.

00:03:59.948 --> 00:04:01.650
Wait a second, let me see that.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.050
- I love that you're
disappointed in an A minus.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.373
- Well, I'm a little mad.

00:04:06.994 --> 00:04:09.532
- My mother would've
thrown babies in the air.

00:04:09.532 --> 00:04:11.476
(audience clapping)

00:04:11.476 --> 00:04:13.680
- Let's see, where does the A
minus that you've been holding

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.780
onto since the 8th grade.
- A minus?

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.703
Wait a second, let me get this.

00:04:18.687 --> 00:04:20.270
Scene two, hang on.

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:23.097
A plus.
- [Kelly] This matters more.

00:04:23.097 --> 00:04:24.067
This matter more.

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:27.141
- It is A plus.
- With a circle!

00:04:27.141 --> 00:04:27.997
With a circle.

00:04:27.997 --> 00:04:31.260
We've got a bunch of super
fans as well in the audience,

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.210
locked and loaded with
questions for all of them.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.680
So we're gonna start with Rachel.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.380
Rachel, what do you wanna know?

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.850
- Hi, cast. (giggles)

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.200
I'm a huge fan of the "Hunger Games".

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.790
I have been since middle school.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.710
In 8th grade, the first movie came out

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.720
and I actually convinced all my teachers

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.620
to take our whole grade
to go see it during class.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.113
If the "Hunger Games"
was happening in 2023,

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.140
who do you think would be the sole victor?

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:58.470
And who do you think would
be the first cannon out?

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:01.593
- How do you not say Josh after that win?

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.163
- I would vote Josh victor.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.401
And I would vote myself for first out.

00:05:08.401 --> 00:05:11.610
(everyone laughing)
I'm so small.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.250
- Singing's not gonna get
you very far in the games.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.080
Well, though it gets you far in the games.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.127
- Yeah, go see the movie and see

00:05:18.127 --> 00:05:19.110
that singing does help.
- Spoiler alert.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.210
- Well, I don't think that
if I was in that position,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:25.220
my first thought would be
like, "I gotta drop some bars."

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.660
- "I gotta drop some bars." (laughs)

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.530
But are y'all competitive?

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:29.730
Hunter, you're competitive, right?

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.290
- I mean, yeah, I'm a Capricorn,

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.270
so I'd say yeah.
- Yeah!

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.103
You know what?

00:05:34.103 --> 00:05:35.720
I vote Hunter, nevermind.
- What?

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.620
- Thank you, Rachel, and also
your outfit is super cute.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.090
Let's move on to Rendy.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.920
So what's your question, Rendy?

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:45.150
- Hello, cast.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.820
Hi, Kelly, and filmmaking team.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.850
Loved the "Hunger Games"
ever since middle school.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:53.970
I did an entire interpretive
dance to "Safe &amp; Sound,"

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.550
to commemorate the first movie's release,

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.600
in front of my entire middle school body.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:03.390
I wanted to know if y'all
were into "Hunger Games"

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:04.950
and had to get sponsorship,

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:06.300
what would be the natural talent

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.193
you'd do to convince everyone?

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.460
- Well, I feel like mine's obvious,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.030
'cause I do it in the
movie, but I guess I'd sing.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.858
I could do a tap dance.

00:06:16.858 --> 00:06:18.660
- [Kelly] Yes!
- And you tap.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.494
- I'd get negative points.
- They'd take money.

00:06:21.494 --> 00:06:23.520
- They'd take money back from me.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:24.787
I don't know, what would you do?

00:06:24.787 --> 00:06:26.490
What would you do?
- I always get complimented

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:27.810
on my whistle for some reason.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:29.250
- He does have a really
good whistle though!

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.170
- Wait, are you one of the
people that can do this thing?

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:34.170
- Not at all, but I can do like a weird,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.090
like, tweety bird, like melodic whistle.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.260
- Do it, please.
- Oh, now I've gotta do it.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.530
Don't I?

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.470
(Tom whistling)

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.030
- Oh, you've got the vibrato.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:43.047
- Yeah, it's like some
weird, I don't know.

00:06:43.047 --> 00:06:44.160
(audience clapping)

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.360
- Money, money, money.
(audience clapping)

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.010
- So much sponsorship.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:51.480
What about you, Hunter?

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.610
Well, I don't know, I feel like,

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.650
I was gonna say draw,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.577
but I saw Josh's drawings

00:06:58.577 --> 00:07:00.420
and I'm like, "I'm out."

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.700
Like, he'd win, so I don't know.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.333
- Now you just go intimidated
by the art that is Josh.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:09.510
Well, obviously Josh would draw.

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:12.420
- I'd probably draw or I
would like, I don't know.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.862
I freestyle rap.

00:07:14.862 --> 00:07:16.380
(everyone laughing)

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.760
I would just try to find the camera

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.710
and look straight at the lens and like,

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.138
you know, give some looks.

00:07:21.138 --> 00:07:24.650
- Charm the sponsor to death.
- See if I could just like...

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:26.190
- The side to side.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:28.650
- I would try everything
under the sun, I don't know.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:29.610
I'm double jointed.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:30.930
I would move my thumb around like this.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.480
- Oh yeah, his thumb is weird, yeah!

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.310
- Wait, do your arms do that too?

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.360
Oh.
- No, it's my thumbs.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:39.320
Sometimes I just.
- It was impressive until, oh!

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:40.950
Oh my God, you look like Gumby.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.360
- I hate it too.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:44.883
Oh, I hate it.
- Ugh, on that note.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.363
Okay, well, we need a commercial break.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:50.970
But first, I've got, that grossed me out.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.310
But first, I've got a little
surprise for everyone here.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.960
Courtesy of our friends at IMAX,

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.640
everyone in the audience is going home

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:57.667
with a pair of tickets to see

00:07:57.667 --> 00:08:00.659
"The "Hunger Games": The
Ballad of Songbirds &amp; Snakes"!

00:08:00.659 --> 00:08:01.492
(audience cheering)

00:08:01.492 --> 00:08:04.370
We're gonna be right back with
What I'm Liking, everybody.

